traditionally the stock traders job has  been to analyze charts company  fundamentals and news announcements  but more often these days they're called  upon to be computer experts  on europe's exchanges about a third of  all orders are now submitted by high  performance computers  these computers are programmed with  algorithms to make buy and sell  decisions thousands of them in  milliseconds in an effort to squeeze  money out of the smallest price swings  the faster the processor the higher the  profits  this is high frequency trading and even  the electronic routing of the data can  make a difference  finance expert hans peter berghoff is  looking into the ways high frequency  trading affects market  high trends trading can execute many  transactions with a speed human beings  can scarcely imagine  we're talking about speeds in the range  of 10 to the power of minus seven or  eight seconds  so a computer can shoot off an order to  the market and then send a replacement  order right after it with 10 to the  power of minus 8 seconds in between  nobody even sees the buy or sell orders  and they're already gone  in worst case scenarios automated  trading can cause sudden drops in the  indices  in 2010 the dow jones plummeted over a  thousand points within minutes  in the united states over half of all  market order decisions are fully  automated if left untouched by human  hands can the system fly out of control  situations keep recurring where the  computers miscalculate the figures  because of some inefficiency or other  and suddenly the market can drop by a  few thousand points  it's critical for both sides to be  continuously adjusted  peter bayer has been trading stocks for  deutsche bank and commerce bank for more  than 30 years  he's just started using a new trading  platform that combines both human and  computer strategies  he thinks it's a bad idea to leave stock  trading up to machines  it's been my experience that you have to  have a very good technical model as a  basis for utilizing all the information  you need to bring whatever trading  skills you already have to bear  that's a basic condition the automated  strategies completely leave out the  emotional aspect of it  there are also strategies that combine  man and machine  but i say machines are unable to learn  to spot a trend over time or changes in  trends on the market  some exchanges prohibit high frequency  trading stuttgart for one  here only human traders can handle the  orders from private investors but that's  hardly viable for the big exchanges in  london and new york  i don't think high frequency trading  should be prohibited  it should be regulated so it won't pose  a danger  but we'll have to put lots of thought  into what kind of regulation that might  be  it's too easy to just require all the  books to be opened and collect all the  information it's going to take a bit  more bureaucratic efforts  new european union guidelines are  tightening the regulations for high  frequency trading  in the end it all comes down to money as  long as computer trading turns a profit  it will be done  you
 

